  "grammar_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Present Tense & Everyday Actions ('inoratidza')",
      "content": "The verb 'inoratidza' uses the present marker '-no-'. In Shona, inserting '-no-' between the subject prefix and the verb stem expresses general truths, everyday habits, or states of mind like 'wanting', 'thinking', and 'knowing'. For example: 'ndinoda' ('I want/like'), 'unofunga' ('you think'), 'vanoti' ('they say'), and 'vanorima' ('they farm').",
      "rule": "[ndi- (I) / u- (you) / a- (he/she) / ti- (we) / va- (they)] + -no- + verb -> Ndinoda (I want), Vanogara (They live)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Colours & Describing Objects in Shona ('muchena')",
      "content": "This passage uses colour vocabulary such as 'muchena'. Shona has three traditional core colour adjectives that change their prefix slightly to match the noun they describe: '-chena' (white/clean, e.g., 'ruvara ruchena' or 'shangu chena'), '-tema / nhema' (black/dark, e.g., 'mota nhema' or 'ruvara rutema'), and '-tsvuku' (red/bright brown, e.g., 'mota tsvuku' or 'ruvara rutsvuku'). For other colours like green ('girinhi'), blue ('bhuruu'), or orange ('orenji'), speakers typically say 'ruvara rwe-' ('the colour of...') followed by the colour name, as in 'ruvara rwegirinhi' (green).",
      "rule": "Core Colours: -chena (white), -tema / nhema (black), -tsvuku (red) | Phrase pattern: ruvara rwe-[colour] (e.g., ruvara rwegirinhi)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Connecting Clauses & Expressing Purpose with 'kuti'",
      "content": "The connector 'kuti' is one of the most useful words in Shona. After verbs of speaking, thinking, or knowing (like 'akati' = said, 'unofunga' = you think, 'ndinoziva' = I know), 'kuti' means 'that'. However, when placed after an action verb, 'kuti' means 'so that / in order to', and the following verb changes its final vowel from '-a' to '-e'! For instance, '-dya' (eat) becomes 'kuti tidye' ('so that we may eat'), and '-wana' (get) becomes 'kuti uwane' ('so that you may get').",
      "rule": "kuti + [Subject Prefix + Verb ending in -e] = 'so that [someone] may...' (e.g., kuti tidye = so that we eat)"
    }
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Mining Tipper Trucks ('Rori Huru') & Zimbabwe's Great Dyke Mineral Belt",
      "content": "Heavy tipper trucks ('marori makuru anozvitutunura') are a familiar sight at mines ('kumamaini') along Zimbabwe's famous Great Dyke—a 550-kilometre geological feature holding some of the world's richest deposits of platinum, chrome, gold, and lithium.",
      "rule": null
    }
  ],
